_Hanois_ L.H.,
Swallows to N.E., 10 P.M. In evening many about west end of island;
also Whitethroats and Willow Wrens. (E., 4.)

_Redcar_, great rush of Ducks to N.W.,
early morning to noon; forty to fifty flocks, thirty to fifty each;
were chiefly Widgeon. (N., moderate.)

_Flambro'_,
Skuas, Gulls, Gannet, etc., to N., in continuous flocks and streams;
also Scoters. (Wind E.N.E., 6.)

_Kentish Knock_ L.V., Crows in hundreds all day to N.N.W.;
one Cock Blackbird to S.W.; and several hundreds of Larks. (N., 7;
rain.)

_Coquet_ L.H., Fieldfares, large flock; one Snow
Bunting; twelve Grey Crows. Large flock of "Mountain" Sparrows;
remained a week. (S.S.E., 9.)

_Langness_ L.H., one Starling on lantern at 3 A.M. One female Blackbird
on lantern at 4.30 A.M., and four Blackbirds at 9 A.M. (Light S.W.;
haze.)

_Eddystone_ L.H.,
Blackbirds, Missel Thrushes, Starlings, and Larks from 3 to 4 A.M.;
many striking. (E., 4; C. M.)

_Nash_
(_E._) L.H., eight *Pied Wagtails killed between 1 and 3 A.M. (N.E., 2;
B. C. M.)

_Selker_ L.V., a
great many Thrushes flying about the light from 9 to 12 P.M. (5, S.W.;
R., misty.)

_Helwick_ L.V., a great number of small birds around the lantern,
also Curlews. Two Warblers and three Whitethroats killed. (N.E., 3;
O. M. R.)

_Skerries Rock_ L.H., several "Flycatchers" striking glass at 12.50
A.M.; two killed. (E.,4; misty.) One Cormorant at 3 P.M. (N.N.E., 5;
C. M.)

_River
Dee_ L.V., flocks of Oyster-catchers passing the whole day. (N.E., 2;
B. C. V.)

_Eddystone_ L.H., numbers of Wheatears, Willow
Wrens, Reed Warblers, and Whitethroats from 9 P.M. (18th) to 3 A.M.;
some striking and several caught. (N. by E., 4; C. P. V.)

_Eddystone_ L.H., a few small birds--Wheatears and
Warblers--flying around the lantern from 9.30 to 12 P.M. (E. by S., 3;
B. C. V.)

_Selker_ L.V., one Lark found dead on deck. (4, S. by W.;
O., misty.) One "Willy the Wisp" rested on deck at 11.30 A.M., then
flew to E. One Thrush flying round ship from 8 to 12 P.M. (Misty.)

_Selker_ L.V., a few Thrushes flying about lantern at
2.30 A.M. (Calm; misty.) One Blackbird going S. at 5.30 A.M. (3, N.W.;
B. M.)

_Chickens Rock_ L.H., great number of Larks, Blackbirds,
Thrushes, Wheatears, and other small birds flying in rays of lantern
from 3 A.M. till daylight. (W. fresh breeze; clear.)

_Holyhead Breakwater_ L.H.,
three Starlings striking glass at 3.30 A.M.; none killed. (N.W., 2;
overcast.) Starlings flying round lantern at 6 A.M. (N.W., 3; cloudy.)

_Longships_ L.H., a good many Larks, Starlings, Goldcrests,
and Chaffinches all night; two "Wrens" and a Lark killed. (E.N.E., 2;
B. C.)

_Chickens Rock_
L.H., three Black Swallows flying S.S.E. at 1 P.M. (N.W. fresh breeze;
haze.)

_Skerries Rock_ L.H.,
Thrushes, Larks, and Chaffinches round light from 8 P.M. to 4 A.M.;
seven killed against glass. (N., 4; clear.)

_Carnarvon Bay_ L.V., very large flocks
of Thrushes and Blackbirds going E. at intervals; two killed. (N.E, 3;
B. C. V.)

_River Dee_ L.V., a
few Thrushes, Larks, and Chaffinches about the light during the night;
one Thrush killed; birds left at daybreak. (S.E., 2; O. M.)

_Bahama Bank_ L.V., number of Thrushes and Larks flying
round lantern from 9 P.M. till 11 P.M.; one Lark killed. (S.S.E., 6;
O. M. K.)

_Chickens Rock_ L.H., number of Rooks coming over from
Ireland, seemed much exhausted, during the day. (E. strong breeze;
haze.)

_Skerries Rock_ L.H., *Jacksnipe shot on island at 2.30 P.M.
A female *Golden-eye killed against glass at 9.15 P.M. (E., 6 to 7;
misty, to C. G. M.)

_Longships_
L.H., Chaffinches and Goldcrests at lantern at 7 P.M. (E.N.E., 3;
cloudy, misty.)

_Helwick_ L.V., a
good many small birds at the lantern; one "Warbler" killed. (E, 2;
O. M. D.)

_South Stack_ L.H., Blackbirds, Thrushes, and Larks around
the lantern, twenty-four killed, from 12.30 A.M. till 3 A.M. (S.E., 2;
O. M.)
